# 10. Event-Driven Architecture

Every significant business action generates an immutable event.

Examples:

CustomerCreated

CustomerUpdated

ProductCreated

SaleRecorded

PaymentReceived

ExpenseRecorded

AIConfirmationAccepted

AIConfirmationRejected

SyncCompleted

NotificationSent

Events are consumed by:

- Timeline Service
- Audit Service
- Notification Service
- AI Memory
- Analytics
- Future Integrations

Business modules never communicate directly when an event-based workflow is appropriate.

---

# 11. Rules Engine

The Rules Engine validates every financial operation before it is committed.

Responsibilities include:

• Validate customer existence

• Validate product existence

• Prevent duplicate transactions

• Prevent negative totals

• Prevent invalid payment amounts

• Prevent overpayments

• Validate required fields

• Validate business ownership

• Validate permissions

If validation fails:

The transaction is rejected.

No partial writes are permitted.

---

# 12. Duplicate Detection Engine

Before creating new records, BizMate AI checks for possible duplicates.

Examples:

"Ade"

"Ade Stores"

"Ade Store"

"Ade Ventures"

may refer to the same customer.

Matching techniques include:

- Exact Match
- Normalised Match
- Phonetic Match
- AI Semantic Match
- Historical Confirmation Match

When confidence is below the configured threshold, the AI requests user confirmation.

Duplicate detection applies to:

- Customers
- Products
- Suppliers (future)
- Categories
- Branches (future)

